1. HYDROGEN AT r = 12 UNDER FORM B: THE ORBIT (GAMEBOARD readings of the design)
   the fan: 2616 directions, one shell per 10 intervals; the width S = 45120 (series H's); Q S = 2887680
   form B on an axis: the pace v = n / (Q S + (T_D / Q) n) with T_D / Q = 110 / 64 = 1.71875 (today's v = n / (Q S + n)); the rows' pace c = 1 / sqrt 3 = 0.5774
   r =  8: E_body = 13.500 entries per shell (the ring mean of the electron's three Nodes), A = 16 Q E r / 10 = 11059.2; today n = 184320.0, p = 338411520, v = 0.06000, T = 838; form B n = 188461.4, p = 346015159, v = 0.05868 (0.9780 of today's, the factor 1 / (1 + 0.72 v) = 0.9586), T = 857 intervals, beta = v / c = 0.1016
   r = 12: E_body = 6.588 entries per shell (the ring mean of the electron's three Nodes), A = 16 Q E r / 10 = 8095.6; today n = 156998.6, p = 288249497, v = 0.05156, T = 1462; form B n = 160012.6, p = 293783192, v = 0.05059 (0.9812 of today's, the factor 1 / (1 + 0.72 v) = 0.9642), T = 1490 intervals, beta = v / c = 0.0876
   the action re-fixed by series H's rule under form B, h_B = 16 p_B(8) = 5536242544 (the registered h = 5414584320, the ratio 1.0225)
   the closure 2 pi p r = j h (DERIVATIONS_BEAM 7.2): at r = 12, j = 4.001 against h_B (whole within 0.001); against the registered h, 4.091; series H's registered p and h give 4.014
   the phase's turn per orbit: j_B turns of the circle, the fraction beyond whole circles 0.001 of a circle = 0.1 steps of 64 per orbit; per quarter orbit (an axis crossing to the next) 1.0003 turns, 0.02 steps beyond whole
   the lumps: T / 10 = 149 kicks per orbit of 2.42 degrees each (series H at r = 12: 146 of 2.5)

2. HYDROGEN: THE PRECESSION PER ORBIT, WHAT THE LAW HAS AND WHAT IT HAS NOT (GAMEBOARD, derived)
   (i) the retarded field of a FIXED source is a steady flux (DERIVATIONS_BEAM 5.3, 12.1): no retardation term acts on the electron; no post-Newtonian term exists in the push (5.3): general relativity's advance has no source here
   (ii) the reader's velocity term (12b.1), the count (1 - n . beta) per direction: on a circle n is radial and beta tangential, n . beta = 0 at first order; the second order is beta^2 = 7.68e-03 of the push, a bound on its precession of 2 pi beta^2 = 2.764 degrees per orbit if it were all radial
   (iii) the fan's departure from the inverse square, the one closed form: the ring mean E_body(r) at r = 10: 8.429, 11: 8.444, 12: 6.588, 13: 6.364, 14: 5.091
       the local exponent k between r = 10 and 12: E ~ r^-1.35; a near-circular orbit under F ~ r^-k has the apsidal angle pi / sqrt(3 - k) = 140.2 degrees, the perihelion moving -79.6 degrees per orbit (k = 2 gives 0)
       the local exponent k between r = 11 and 13: E ~ r^-1.69; a near-circular orbit under F ~ r^-k has the apsidal angle pi / sqrt(3 - k) = 157.5 degrees, the perihelion moving -45.0 degrees per orbit (k = 2 gives 0)
       the local exponent k between r = 12 and 14: E ~ r^-1.67; a near-circular orbit under F ~ r^-k has the apsidal angle pi / sqrt(3 - k) = 156.2 degrees, the perihelion moving -47.5 degrees per orbit (k = 2 gives 0)
       the local exponent k between r = 10 and 14: E ~ r^-1.50; a near-circular orbit under F ~ r^-k has the apsidal angle pi / sqrt(3 - k) = 146.9 degrees, the perihelion moving -66.2 degrees per orbit (k = 2 gives 0)
   (iv) the whole kicks (one shell per 10 intervals) have no closed form: series H's loops were eccentric and precessing at every radius; registered, not pinned

3. HYDROGEN: WHAT THE DETECTOR AT THE PROTON READS (the detector's EXPECTED readings, derived; the criteria of series H beside them)
   the electron releases one ray per in-plane heading every 10 intervals from its three Nodes; the ray on -X from its middle Node (z = c) reaches the proton's Node after 12 Links x 1.7188 = 20.6 intervals
   a click at the proton at every crossing of an axis of the proton by the electron's middle Node: four crossings per orbit, T / 4 = 373 intervals apart (the orbital frequency 1 / T = 6.71e-04 per interval, 4 / T = 2.68e-03 clicks per interval); the electron holds a Node for about 1 / v = 20 intervals at a crossing, so one or two releases fall on the axis: 4 to 8 clicks per orbit
   the first click: the electron starts on the +x axis; its first release at age 10 reaches the proton at about 31; then about 403, 776, 1148, 1521 (T within 15 percent: 1267 to 1714 between returns to the same axis)
   the phase of the clicks: the ray carries the electron's phase at release (no phase_per_link on e); a whole closure reads the same step at the same axis every orbit within the drift +0.1 steps per orbit; successive axes differ by j_B / 4 turns = 0.0 steps beyond whole
   the return (the orbit criterion of series H): within r / 4 = 3 Links of the start at the closing of the angle, T within 15 percent of 1490

4. HELIUM: THE NUCLEUS, THE TWO ELECTRONS, THE FLUXES (GAMEBOARD, derived)
   the nucleus: binding-v1's square (p 1834 + held nuclear 1 + bond 2, n 1837 + the same; the proton's charge 4), FIXED at the four Nodes about (c + 1/2, c + 1/2, c), each releasing on the fan by the world's release [1, 18360]: 0.09989 and 0.10005 shells per interval
   the electrons: the register's electron (charge -15, content 1836) at the offsets (13, 1, 0) and (-12, 0, 0) from c, point-symmetric about the square's centre, r = 12.510, each releasing every 10 intervals on the fan's band |c| <= 2 (264 directions) and the four in-plane headings: every line of the fan that reaches the partner's three Nodes lies in the band (the partner's entries per shell 7 from the band against 7 from the whole fan), and no ray of the band steps from an outer Node of the electron's own set into its middle Node
   the push products per ray, |rho_e rho_k - 1|: a proton 61 (inward), a neutron 1 (inward, gravity alone), the partner electron rho_e^2 - 1 = 224 (outward)
   at the start Nodes, exact (the entries per shell the electron's three Nodes receive from each source at its offset): the nucleus's four sources, weighted and projected on the centre's direction, 743.43; the partner at 2r, 7 entries per shell, x 224 = 1568
   on the ring (the orbit's mean): the nucleus 811.64 = (2 x 61 + 2 x 1) x E_body(12.51) with E_body = 6.545; the partner 224 x E_body(25.02) = 224 x 1.300 = 291.20; the net inward 520.44 (hydrogen's 16 x 6.588 = 105.41 at r = 12)
   with series H's proton (charge [1, 1]) in place of binding-v1's: the nucleus (2 x 16 + 2 x 1) x E_body = 222.55 against the partner's 291.20: the net -68.65, OUTWARD: no orbit, the pair repelled (the register's electron carries 15 times the proton's charge per unit of content, so two electrons repel each other 14 times as strongly as a proton holds one)

5. HELIUM: THE ORBIT AND THE PARTNER'S RETARDED DRAG (GAMEBOARD, derived; the drag DERIVATIONS_BEAM 12.1's first-order term)
   the circular orbit under form B with the net inward push: A = Q r (net) / 10 = 41668.2; n = 384530.1, p = 705997315, v = 0.10836, T = 725 intervals, beta = 0.1877
   the closure against hydrogen's h_B: j = 2 pi p r / h_B = 10.024 (whole within 0.024 of 10); the phase's turn per orbit 1.5 steps beyond whole
   the partner's rays reach the electron from where the partner was 2r / c = 43 intervals earlier, 4.70 Links behind along its orbit: the repulsion tilts backward by the angle whose sine is beta (12.1), a tangential component against the motion of beta x 224 E(2r) per interval = 349.789 per unit of content, against the net inward 3330.793: 0.105 of it
   over one orbit the drag takes 253728 of n = 384530: 0.66 of the momentum per orbit; the reader's factor on the partner's rays (1 - beta^2) = 0.9648 and on the nucleus's 1 at first order
   so the diametral pair is not a closed orbit of the law: the pinned reading is the DECAY, the electrons' momenta falling by about half per orbit and the pair spiralling in (or apart, if the kicks scatter them) within two turns; a closed orbit at r = 12.5 would refute 12.1's drag

6. THE HOST COST, ESTIMATED FROM SERIES H'S RUNS (host numbers, not the model's)
   series H: 19 to 87 s per world on four cores, the proton's 262 rays per interval, records a few hundred megabytes per world (the bohr README); r12 (53^3, 7400 intervals) about 60 s
   hydrogen_r12 under form B: the same world with 7500 intervals: about 60 s, a few hundred megabytes
   helium_r12: four nucleons on the whole fan and two electrons on the band, 1100 rays per interval (4.2 times series H's), 55^3, 3700 intervals: about 4.2 x 60 x 3700 / 7400 = 126 s, about one gigabyte of records
